  <scopecontent id="c5c40e7b3ffabd77e6fe3b61d567091a">
    <head>Scope and Contents</head>
<p>This collection documents the life and career of Paul Webbink including but not limited to his committee, meeting, and policy records of the Social Science Research Council, personal and professional correspondence, as well as papers, reports, article reprints, and a selection of volumes from his personal library.</p>  </scopecontent>
  <bioghist id="14312ff959f7c575c86bfe7655119e41">
    <head>Biographical / Historical</head>
<p>Paul Webbink joined the Social Science Research Council in 1936 and, from 1937-1943 served as Director of the council's Committee on Social Security, which organized a program which provided essential information to the United States government to help the enactment and implemention of the Social Security Act passed in 1935.</p><p>From 1943 to 1948 Webbink served as the head of the SSRC Washington D.C. office during which time he worked closely with various government agencies to encourage and advocate for wartime service by social scientists.</p><p>From 1948 until his retirement in 1970, Webbink served as Vice President of the SSRC, with much of his efforts focusing in the areas of labor market research, income distribution, population and the census, as well as economic growth and development. As vice president of the council since 1948, Mr. Webbink coordinated work of the new council committee on labor market research, extended council research to economic growth and stability.</p><p>Prior to his work at the SSRC, Webbink was a research assistant to Senator Robert. M. La Follette Jr. of Wisconsin, from 1927 to 1933, and a statistician with the Federal Emergency Relief Administration from 1933 to 1936.</p>  </bioghist>
